Apologies for the late response on this one! I'm not exactly sure what is going on here as the df in your second model should be 0 since it is a fully saturated model. In the case of a three-indicator, common factor model, Q_SNP is actually just the model chi-square of the common pathways model (your first model) in which the SNP is predicting the common factor. This is because the independent pathways model (your second model) in which you specify the SNP to directly predict the three indicators will perfectly fit the model, so you'd be subtracting out 0 df and a model chi-square of 0 from the common pathways model.

That said, something is off with the degrees of freedom printing as 5 for your second model. It could be a package bug, but it's hard to tell because the model you list is labeled as model2, and userGWAS specifies running a model: modelD_Q. If you aren't accidentally passing a different model to userGWAS then what you write for model2, then if you could send the first 50 rows of the sumstats output and the LDSCoutput then I can do a line-by-line run and see why you are getting a df of 5. My e-mail to send that output to is: andrew.g...@colorado.edu

Thanks!
 -Andrew

